## 1. What this round actually changed

### 1.1 A far-region physical hypothesis is now *proved*, not assumed

Previously the far region carried the hypothesis "for some axis
`|sin(p_μ/2)| ≥ 87/128`" as a conditional physical input. It is now a theorem:

* `two_mul_le_sinAtom` — Jordan's inequality in the lattice normalisation,
  `sin(π x) ≥ 2x` on `[0,1/2]`;
* `abs_sinAtom_ge_of_far` — hence `|sin(π x)| ≥ 4/5` whenever `2/5 ≤ |x| ≤ 1/2`,
  which is *sharper* than the constructive constant `87/128`
  (`abs_sinAtom_ge_87_128_of_far` records the weaker form);
* `phatSq_floor_far` — the far denominator floor `p̂² ≥ 64/25`, sharper than the
  previous `7569/4096`;
* `farAxis_of_not_nearBox` — the constructive axis selection.

No rational π-enclosure, no interval arithmetic, no long-running solver call is
needed: the whole far denominator lane is three short proofs.

### 1.2 The cell functional is no longer an interface

The shell bound used to be stated for an abstract positive linear functional
with assumed additivity, monotonicity, constant rule and near/far split. In
`ShellBudget.lean` the functional is the actual Lebesgue integral over
`[0,1/2]^4 ⊂ (Fin 4 → ℝ)`, and

* `volume_brillouinCell` : the cell has measure `1/16`;
* `volume_farCellShell` : the far shell has measure `1/16 − r⁴`;
* `shell_lower_bound` : pointwise region bounds integrate to a cell bound.

The split, additivity and monotonicity are theorems about `volume`, not
hypotheses.

### 1.3 The A1a physical input is now a single scalar inequality

`TransverseFormFactor.lean` proves `|K_{μν}(p)| ≤ p̂²` for the literal Wilson
inverse propagator `K_{μν} = δ_{μν} p̂² − p̂_μ p̂_ν`
(`abs_wilsonInversePropagator_le`), so for a transversely factorised joint
numerator `N_{μν} = K_{μν} Π`:

* near box: `|Π(p)| ≤ A p̂²` with `A ≤ 1/40` ⟹ per-orbit density `≤ 2/5`
  (`near_perOrbit_bound_of_formFactor`);
* far shell: `|Π(p)| ≤ 25/128` ⟹ per-orbit density `≤ 25/64`
  (`far_perOrbit_bound_of_formFactor`); alternatively a numerator sup `≤ 1`
  through the proved denominator floor
  (`far_perOrbit_bound_of_numerator_sup`).

Two separate physical hypotheses (near cofactor data `m/c ≤ 2/5`; far axis sine
gap plus numerator sup) have therefore been replaced by **one** hypothesis on
**one** scalar function.

### 1.4 A1a closure modulo that scalar bound

`A1aClosure.A1a_closure_of_formFactor` gives, from the form-factor bounds alone,

```
−splitCost(2/5)  ≤  ∫_{[0,1/2]^4} ( 4 d₁ + 6 d₂ + 4 d₃ + d₄ )
```

with the cancellation-preserving combination bounded *before* integration and

```
splitCost(2/5) = (2/5)^4 · 6 + (1/16 − (2/5)^4) · (375/64) = 236679/640000
               = 0.3698…  <  11/12 = shell budget      (margin > 2×).
```

### 1.4b The split radius is not fine-tuned

`RadiusRobustness.splitCostGeneral_lt_budget`: using the radius-dependent floor
`p̂_μ² ≥ 16 r²` (Jordan again, at arbitrary radius) instead of the fixed
`64/25`, the total split cost stays strictly below `11/12` for *every*
`r ∈ [1/4, 1/2]`.  The working value `r = 2/5` is therefore a convenience, not a
tuned parameter; at that value the two floors coincide
(`splitCostGeneral_two_fifths`).

### 1.5 A1b: the contraction package, with the boundary case settled

`BetaObserver.lean` proves, for observer data `(O, T, κ, L, B)`:

* `observer_square_iterate` — the observed history is the history of the
  observation (the same-object audit, as a theorem);
* `historyRemainder_bound` — `|R_hist| ≤ L γ B`, `γ = 1/(1−κ)`, **uniformly in
  the number of shells**;
* `historyRemainder_bound_of_products` — the fallback when pointwise `κ < 1`
  fails: any bound `S` on the partial sums of the response products
  `∏_{i<n} κ_i` gives `|R_hist| ≤ L S B`;
* `exists_fixedPoint_of_contraction` / `historyRemainder_bound_complete` — on a
  complete observer space the fixed point is produced by the contraction rather
  than assumed, so `hfix` is not an extra physical hypothesis;
* `kappa_one_no_finite_budget` — at `κ = 1` the budget genuinely fails: for the
  unit shift on `ℝ` with `β = id` (exactly `κ = 1`, `L = 1`) the history sums
  are unbounded. The boundary produces no fake finite `B`.

### 1.6 End-to-end Row A1

`A1Closure.A1_beta_pos`: given the A1a form-factor bounds, the A1b observer
data with `L γ B ≤ 1/2`, and the shell decomposition
`β = (11/24) C_A + R_local + R_hist` with `R_local` the cell integral above,

```
β  ≥  b₋(G) = (11/24) C_A(G) − 236679/640000 − 1/2  >  0   for every SU(N), N ≥ 2.
```

`A1_beta_pos_nonvacuous` instantiates the whole hypothesis package, so the
theorem is not reached through a contradictory hypothesis set.

